{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>You shall not take the name of HaShem your Elohim in vain.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Shavuot 39a) that the whole world trembled when the Holy One, blessed be He, said 'לֹא תִשָּׂא' etc., and the reason for this is because it is written (Hosea 4:2–3) 'swearing and lying and killing and stealing,' 'therefore the earth shall mourn.' And from this verse our Rabbis of blessed memory learned (ibid. 39a) that for all transgressions punishment is exacted from him alone, but for a false oath punishment is exacted from him and from the entire world. If so, it is fitting that the whole world should tremble when He said 'לֹא תִשָּׂא,' for all are affected by the punishment. And the reason for this is made clear in the language of 'לֹא תִשָּׂא,' which is the language of lifting up, for this matter is like one who lifts a tree and raises it up — surely all the branches tremble along with the tree. So too the one who swears by His name, blessed be He, because all existing things depend on His name, blessed be He, and the one who lifts up His name, blessed be He, is as though he lifted and raised up all the branches that are borne with it and joined to it — that is, all existent beings above and below. And if so, it is just that all of them should tremble and that the punishment should touch them all. Therefore it is said here 'כִּי לֹא יְנַקֶּה HaShem' — the word 'כִּי' is like giving a reason, and it is as though it were a definitive statement that it is impossible to absolve him, for it is not given to forgiveness at all. And this is the matter we have spoken concerning the name of HaShem, for the Holy One, blessed be He, forgives only in matters between a person and his fellow, but not in matters between a person and his fellow. And therefore this one who touched the trunk of the holy tree and caused punishment to all the branches — the upper chambers with the lower chambers — how could the Holy One, blessed be He, forgive him? And the existing beings who received the punishment on his account — where are they, that he could appease them? Could he go from one end of the heavens to the other, and from the depths of the earth to the height of the firmament, to appease all the existing beings among the higher ones and the lower ones, seeing that he touched them all? Therefore it is said 'כִּי לֹא יְנַקֶּה HaShem' — the matter is not in the hand of the Holy One, blessed be He, to forgive this sin, for it is not given to forgiveness at all." "And there is no need to elaborate further on the severity of an oath, and on the severity of invoking the name of Heaven for nothing, for this is well known and widely publicized. And the length of this exile will prove that this sin alone suffices for the length of this bitter exile, on account of the common habit upon our tongues — for the name of Heaven is fluent in the mouth of every person of Israel; he does not tremble or fear at its mention. And for all transgressions between a person and his fellow there is a court below, but for this dreadful sin there is no court and no judge." "And so the prophet Isaiah said (Isaiah 48:1–2): 'Hear this, O house of Jacob, who are called by the name of Israel, and from the waters of Judah they came forth, who swear by the name of HaShem and make mention of the Elohim of Israel, not in truth and not in righteousness. For from the holy city they are called, and upon the Elohim of Israel they lean — HaShem of Hosts is His name.' He says: they should have been careful in mentioning His great name, blessed be He, for their name is like the name of their master — for they are the house of Jacob, who was called by the name Israel, for in it is included the name אֵ״ל, 'יִשְׂרָ־אֵל,' and 'from the waters of Judah they came forth,' for in him are included all the letters of the special Name, and so the name אֵ״ל and the special Name are contained within them. And even so, they swear by these two names, 'by the name of HaShem and make mention of the Elohim of Israel, not in truth' etc., for concerning these two names it was said 'לֹא תִשָּׂא אֶת שֵׁם HaShem אֱלֹהֶיךָ לַשָּׁוְא.' And if you should say that the reason they are not careful with these two names is because they are not stored up and esteemed in their eyes, concerning this he said 'for from the holy city they are called' — for they trace their lineage after the holy city that is called by the name of HaShem, as it is written (Ezekiel 48:35) 'וְשֵׁם הָעִיר מִיּוֹם HaShem שָׁמָּה,' 'and upon the Elohim of Israel they lean,' to say 'HaShem of Hosts is His name' — that He is HaShem over all the hosts of Israel. And if so, in every place they trace their lineage after His name, blessed be He, and so all the names are stored up and esteemed in their eyes, and even so they are not careful with them and invoke the name 'not in truth and not in righteousness,' corresponding to the two occurrences of 'לַשָּׁוְא' that He mentioned here — one meaning 'for nothing' and one meaning 'falsehood.' Now, truth is the opposite of falsehood, and what is written 'not in righteousness' means 'for nothing,' for the one who swears in court has intent to justify his claims, and concerning this it was said (Deuteronomy 10:20) 'וּבִשְׁמוֹ תִּשָּׁבֵעַ,' but the one who invokes the name for nothing does not intend to justify his claims, but rather to justify his words of emptiness, and this is called 'not in righteousness.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "The clause 'for the Holy One, blessed be He, forgives only in matters between a person and his fellow, but not in matters between a person and his fellow' appears contradictory as it stands in the Hebrew; the Hebrew may be slightly garbled — the logic of the passage requires 'matters between him and the Omnipresent' in the first clause. Translated hyper-literally as found."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:21:58.111789688Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}